From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_STAGE_CACHE=1: translated shader stages reused across pipelines that differ only in fixed state (Djihads80/Eglantine d1195b7), capped by EDEN_UP_STAGE_CACHE_MIB (32) with least-recently-used eviction; EDEN_UP_STAGE_CACHE_VERIFY=1 rebuilds and compares; all off by default

Djihads80/Eglantine d1195b7 (EDEN_NX2_STAGE_CACHE there, on by default): pipelines that differ
only in fixed state translate the same programs again. In an Ocarina of Time cold boot 64% of the
stages gave SPIR-V the same program had already given, about half of the translation and emission
time; 0 differences in its verify mode on NVIDIA (1,276 stages reused) and RADV (106 reused). On
the PS5 translation is the stall at a first visit: 8-18 ms a pipeline on the GPU thread, up to
280 ms for a mesh pipeline.

How it works (as upstream): a native stage is translated through a forwarding environment that
records the reads translation makes (constant buffer values, storage pointer checks, texture
types and formats, viewport transform, HLE replacements), in order. Another pipeline reuses the
translation when its own environment answers those reads the same way, replayed in order, and
the program, slot, environment constants and recompiler settings match. Translation is
deterministic, so every read replayed up to a difference is one the translation would make too:
the live environment ends up holding exactly the answers its own translation would have asked
for, which is what goes to vulkan.bin. The emitted SPIR-V, program metadata and outgoing
bindings are reused when the runtime info and incoming bindings are byte-for-byte equal;
otherwise the stage is translated again and emitted. A merged VertexA/VertexB pair, Switch 1
programs and layer emulation sources always translate.

Changed for this tree:
- Off unless EDEN_UP_STAGE_CACHE is exactly "1" (Common::PeSwitch); the upstream name is not read.
- Memory: EDEN_UP_STAGE_CACHE_MIB=<n> (8-4096, default 32; upstream 256 MiB and starts over).
  Past the cap the least recently used translations go until a quarter of the cap is free; at
  most 8 translations per program and slot and 8 emitted variants per translation (the least
  recently used goes); two workers that translated the same stage at once keep one copy; a
  bad_alloc in a pipeline build or in the cache empties it. The driver's compiler running out of
  memory on a worker is not seen here, so the cap is the guard: a translation and each emitted
  variant hold about 10 KB of shader info besides their SPIR-V, the boot translates thousands of
  stages while every worker compiles, and the PS5 has a few hundred MB free.
- A reused stage replays the lowest and highest program address its translation read, so a
  program read past its known size still keeps the pipeline out of vulkan.bin
  (has_unbound_instructions) instead of a record that fails at the next boot (upstream skips it).
- Our CreateGraphicsPipeline: the OoT visual workaround runs before the translation is kept (and
  again when a reused translation is re-emitted), the known-broken-pattern check is skipped for a
  reused stage (it passed when translated), guest shader dumps turn the cache off for that build,
  mesh-as-compute and the noperspective fields are in the runtime key.
- Reads are told apart exactly (upstream keeps a 64-bit hash of each); the signature also holds
  the downscale setting and the OoT workaround state; verify also compares the shader infos.
- Log line every 10 s while it works: stages reused whole, translations reused, translated, held
  MiB, evicted, lookups with no signature held (and of those, the ones whose program is held under
  another signature: a disk record's environment answers HasHLEMacroState and IsProprietaryDriver
  by other rules than a live one, so boot entries may never match a lookup during play), and
  which kind of read made a reuse fail.
- The three switch names are in the shader preparation's stamp list (vk_pipeline_prep.cpp): set,
  an arm gets a store of its own (one preparation at its first boot); unset, the stamp is the same.
  _MIB and _VERIFY change no output but stay listed on purpose: each arm's first boot is then a
  cold preparation (the prep time and peak memory a test compares), and a verify run checks every
  stage the boot reuses instead of finding the store already full.

With the shader preparation (EDEN_UP_SHADER_PREP) and the shader set (EDEN_UP_SHADER_SET):
- Boot: the preparation translates every disk record on the workers, and with the shader set every
  recipe's predicted record too. These share programs heavily (the shader set's canonical keys
  found up to 20 keys for the same shaders in Ocarina), so this is where the cache saves most:
  fewer translations per preparation, less worker time beside the guest cores. The kept SPIR-V
  and infos are the same bytes either way.
- A pipeline made from the kept store is not translated and does not ask the cache. The boot's
  sample of kept records translated again for comparison builds like any pipeline: it asks and
  fills the cache (stages this build translated, so the comparison still holds).
- A pipeline that is not kept (a new fixed-state combination, a refused or struck-out record)
  translates on the GPU thread as before, and its stages can come from what the boot translated:
  the recorded answers (a disk record's, or a predicted record's guessed ones) are replayed
  against the game's state now and must all be the same. The kept check (AnswersHoldFor) is
  looser: texture handle and pointer words may differ. The miss counts by read kind tell whether
  that rule is worth adding here later.
- The prep verify build (EDEN_UP_SHADER_PREP_VERIFY=1) never takes a stage from the cache
  (ShaderPrep::Stages::no_stage_cache), so it stays a check of real translations, and of what
  the cache gave the kept store at boot.
- Memory: the boot likely fills the cache up to the cap and least-recently-used eviction keeps it
  there during play; the cap is the cost to watch on the PS5 (held MiB in the log line).

Unset or anything but "1": no stage is looked up, recorded or kept, the build runs the same
translation and emission calls in the same order, and the prep stamp is the same.
